  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery had the center of an upper ridge over the TX\ngulf coast as an amplified upper trough moved towards the northern \nRockies. A couple low amplitude shortwaves were noted in the \nsouthwest flow over the central plains. At the surface, high pressure\nwas located over the Upper Midwest with low pressure along the lee \nof the Rockies. This pattern has favored continued southeasterly low \nlevel flow and moisture transport north. \n\nThe initial wave and associated precip is moving east northeast out \nof eastern KS as another shortwave comes out of west TX. This should \nbring a lull in precip this afternoon before the next round of \nshowers and storms move though overnight. Mid level lapse rates are \nprogged to remain relatively shallow limiting instability for \nstorms. So the main risk looks to be localized heavy rain as a very \nmoist airmass remains over the plains. Think the storms within the \nsouthwesterly flow should remain progressive enough to mitigate a \nwidespread flooding risk. \n\nFor Saturday, the question is whether we can clear out and warm up \nfor thunderstorms to redevelop. Mid level lapse rates could steepen \nacross central KS. If temps can warm into the lower 80s, some \nmoderate instability of around 3000 J/kg of MUCAPE could help spark \nconvection in the afternoon. At this time, there is a fair amount of \nuncertainty whether we will clear out as the RAP tends to hang onto \nlow clouds well into the afternoon. Think the SWODY2 trend of \nshifting the risk more towards south central KS is reasonable given \nthe chance for clouds to linger and limit destabilization. If things \ndo clear out, marginal bulk shear of 20 to 30KT may limit storm \norganization to multi-cellular clusters and a wind risk. \n\nShortwave ridging is progged to redevelop over the central and \nsouthern plains for Sunday and Monday with a break from the rain \nchances. The main question in the forecast is how an upper trough \nwill evolve coming out of the southwestern U.S. next week. 12Z \noperational solutions seem to be centering around the idea of a \ncutoff low slowly lifting through the plains mid to late week next \nweek. However the 00Z ensembles were nearly evenly split on a close \nversus a more phased open solution. So there remains a fair amount \nof spread in the predictability of this pattern for Tuesday through \nFriday. Nevertheless the signal for heavy rainfall is seen in the \nensembles with the GEFS and ECMWF ensemble indicating PWs will be 2 \nto 3 standard deviations above normal. The operational GFS progs PWs \nto be over 2 inches by 12Z Wednesday. This is likely due to the fact \nthe models are absorbing the remnants of Polo into the upper trough \nand bringing that tropical fetch of moisture into the plains. Most \nguidance points to Tuesday night through Wednesday night as the best \ntiming for precip, but if the close solution prevails a slower \nprogression to the system with cyclogenesis could occur causing high \nprecip chances to persist through Friday. This leads to the heavy \nrainfall risk next week. The NBM shows widespread 2 to 4 inches of \nrain through Thursday. The 12Z GFS and ECMWF suggest 3 to 5 inches \nare possible by Friday evening. Widespread rains of these magnitudes \neven spread out over a few days could lead to flooding. So will have \nto continue to monitor the trends with the storm system for next \nweek. Temperatures look to remain rather seasonal as the mean \nwesterlies remain across the northern tier of the county with no \nobvious surge of cold air south seen in the operational or ensemble \nsolutions. Think the NBM/WPC forecast looks reasonable through next \nweek. There could be some adjustments to temps depending on how \nnext week's storm system progresses east.",
